# Implementation Decisions

Choices made while writing `loop/implementation-guide.md` and `loop/checklist.md`.
These are implementation-level (tooling, file layout, code shape) and do not change
any behavior fixed by `loop/specification.md`. Each can be overridden by the
developer; if overridden, update the guide, checklist, and (if relevant) the spec.

## I1. Test layout: colocated `*.test.ts` next to source

Vitest's default and lowest-friction layout. `src/sim/lane.ts` →
`src/sim/lane.test.ts`. Keeps a unit and its tests in one place (spec §12.1's
"files that change together live together"). Integration/acceptance tests that
span modules live in `src/__tests__/` (e.g. replay, anti-camping, economy).

## I2. Node version pinned to 22 LTS via `.nvmrc` and `engines`

Spec §12.3/§13 require the golden replay to be stable on "the same JS
engine/build" and CI to pin the Node version. Chose Node 22 (active LTS as of
2026). Recorded in `.nvmrc` and `package.json` `engines.node`.

## I3. Entity storage: array-of-structs with object pools

Enemies, shots, and particles are plain objects held in arrays, each carrying
`prevLane/prevDepth` and `currLane/currDepth` for interpolation (spec §12.3).
Freed objects return to a per-type pool so the render/sim hot loops allocate
nothing (spec §11.1). Chosen over struct-of-arrays for readability; entity counts
are tiny (≤ ~64), so AoS cost is irrelevant.

## I4. Config injection via a single `GameConfig` object

`createSim(config: GameConfig, seed: number)` (spec §12.2/D41). `GameConfig`
bundles the difficulty anchors (§8.2), tuning constants (§8.3), score table (§7),
and geometry data (§4). The app builds it from the live data modules; tests build
it from a frozen snapshot checked into `src/__tests__/fixtures/`.

## I5. RNG: mulberry32, one instance in sim state, explicit derivation

`sim/rng.ts` exports `makeRng(seed)` returning `{ next(): number, state(): number,
setState(n) }`. The sim holds exactly one RNG; the renderer holds a *separate*
mulberry32 seeded independently (spec §11.1) so visuals never perturb sim
determinism. RNG internal state is part of the sim hash (spec §12.2).

## I6. Fixed-point-free but transcendental-free sim math

Per spec §12.3, sim math uses only `+ − × ÷`, comparison, `Math.round/floor/ceil`,
integer/bit ops, and RNG. No `Math.sqrt`/`sin`/`cos` in `sim/` outside the
projection module; the ESLint `no-restricted-properties` rule enforces it.
Distance checks use squared magnitudes. (Lane math is 1-D on `depth`, so this is
easy to honor.)

## I7. Types: branded-free plain numbers with clear names

`type Lane = number` (fractional for rimPos, integer for a resolved lane index),
`type Depth = number` (0..1). No nominal typing/branding — the spec's rules are
simple enough that named `number` aliases plus tests are sufficient and keep the
code readable (global CLAUDE.md: "boring solution", maintainability over
cleverness).

## I8. State machine as a discriminated `GameState` union + pure reducer

`sim/state.ts`: `type Phase = 'TITLE' | 'LEVEL_SELECT' | 'PLAYING' | 'GET_READY'
| 'WARP' | 'GAME_OVER' | 'HIGH_SCORE_ENTRY'` and a pure `step(state, input) =>
{ state, events }`. Transitions live in one file so the §13 "every transition and
no others" test can target it directly. Pause is NOT here (app-layer, D19).

## I9. Events: a flat `SimEvent[]` returned per tick

`tick()` returns `{ hash?, events: SimEvent[] }`. Events are a discriminated union
(`{type:'enemyKilled', kind, depth, lane}`, `{type:'playerShot'}`,
`{type:'pulseTelegraph'}`, …). Audio and particles consume events; the sim never
imports audio/render. Keeps the sim pure (spec §12.2).

## I10. Build/test scripts

`package.json`: `dev` (vite), `build` (vite build), `test` (vitest run),
`test:watch` (vitest), `typecheck` (tsc --noEmit), `lint` (eslint). `npm run check`
runs typecheck + lint + test (the CI gate, spec §15.10).

## I11. Phased build order (guide sections) — sim-first, inside-out

Build the pure sim and persist layers first (fully unit-tested with no DOM), then
render/audio/input, then app wiring, then integration + acceptance. This lets the
riskiest logic (spec §13's test list) be verified before any browser code exists,
matching the spec's separation of concerns. Phases are the checklist's top-level
groups.

## I12. Commit granularity: one commit per completed task (green tests)

Per the writing-plans skill and the build prompt (`loop/prompt-3-build`): each
task ends with passing tests and a commit. The checklist item is checked off in
the same commit that completes it.

## I14. Save data is injected into `createSim` as `InitialSave` (impl-review round 1)

The sim must decide level-select bounds (§8.5) and high-score qualification (§10)
from persisted data, but may not read `localStorage` (purity, §12.2). So
`createSim(config, seed, initialSave?)` takes a read-only `{ maxLevelReached,
highScores }`; the sim owns the in-session table and the app persists its final
state back (Tasks 3.1 / 11.1). Keeps the sim pure and the qualification transition
sim-owned.

## I15. `—` difficulty cells are `number | null` (impl-review round 1)

`DifficultyAnchor.spikeH` and `.pulse` are `number | null`; `null` encodes the
spec's `—` cells, normalized by `paramsForLevel` to the column's first defined
value (§8.1). Avoids implementers diverging on `undefined`/`NaN`/prefilled numbers.

## I16. Lint purity rules active from Phase 1; hash module created in Phase 3 (impl-review round 1)

The sim-purity/engine-stability ESLint rules are turned on in Task 0.1 (not deferred
to Phase 12) so they guard sim code as it is written; Task 12.4 only proves they
fire. The state-hash module is created in Task 3.1 (when `SimState` exists) and
extended by each later task ("add to the hash as you go"), so that instruction
refers to a module that exists; Task 12.1 audits it and Task 12.3 is the automated
completeness backstop.

## I13. `codex-review` after each task during build

The build prompt (`loop/prompt-3-build`) asks for codex + internal review of each
implemented item. The guide's per-task Definition of Done includes running the
`/codex-review` skill (or `codex exec`) and addressing medium+ findings before
checking the item off.
